4.7.2
---
- A patch that opens two libraries, which is what a patch means in this
  project: qxcupertino and qxharmony are the third and fourth control sets,
  the families the 4.7 layer had promised but not yet filled, and the gallery
  that shows the control sets grows from two pages to four so each family has
  a page of its own. Every library's public API only gained declarations this
  time - not one signature changed, nothing was removed and nothing was
  renamed - so the compatibility promise of the 4.7 series still holds and the
  project version is a patch
- qxcupertino 0.1.0. A new library, opened with the family's five scales - the
  corners, the type ramp, the elevation levels, the durations and curves, the
  semantic colours - and eight controls on them: the switch whose knob follows
  the hand, the slider, the segmented control whose pill stands under the
  chosen segment, the button in its three levels, the activity indicator of
  eight spokes fading round a centre, the text field that a search field is one
  property away from, the list section and its tiles, and the alert dialog
  whose answers answer by role. One defect left the door with it: the
  segmented control's pill kept the light face on a dark page, and read white
  on white
- qxharmony 0.1.0. A new library, opened the same way - five scales and the
  vocabulary they fix - with four controls: the switch whose knob answers the
  hand rather than waiting for the release, the slider whose bubble floats up
  to say the value while it is held, the card that stands on its shadow, and
  the button in this family's three levels. The slider also asks for the height
  its own drawing needs now, which is a sizeHint change its CHANGELOG owns
- qxfluent 0.1.2. The navigation view gains its second shape, the one WinUI's
  navigation view has: a destination can hold destinations under it, folded and
  opened from the arrow, the keyboard or setCurrentIndex, and folded to its
  narrowest the pane hands what stands under an icon out to a panel beside it.
  The command faces - the bar and the two buttons that stand alone - draw from
  one place now, and the bar takes the shape the buttons brought: its chevron
  was a shade deeper than theirs and its seam a shade lighter, both differences
  of history rather than of promise. Two defects are fixed in their own
  entries: a window material asked of a window that already had a qxwindow
  agent made a second agent and the caption stopped moving the window, and the
  panel a folded pane opens stayed up until one of its own rows was chosen
  instead of ending on a press anywhere else
- qxmaterial 0.1.2. The navigation rail's column travels inside the room it is
  given: destinations past the bottom padding used to be invisible while their
  rectangles still caught a press aimed at the padding - invisible and
  clickable was the worst of both halves of off the rail - and now the wheel
  moves the column a destination a notch, the keyboard keeps what it walks to
  in sight, and what stands outside the room is clipped and beyond the pointer
  alike. The top app bar no longer puts the keyboard's place on the first
  trailing action the moment it is added, which is where the permanent ring
  that mark wore came from
- qxwindow 0.3.3. WindowAgentWidget gains hostWidget(), the answer to "which
  window has this agent taken over" that setup() could only give once and in
  one direction. An object holding a window can tell one agent on it from
  another now, which is what asking a window material of a window that draws
  its own caption needs
- qxcore 0.2.0, qxtheme 0.2.2, qxcontrols 0.1.0, qxribbon 0.14.2, qxdock 0.3.3,
  qxplugin 0.3.0 and qxapp 0.2.1 keep the numbers they had. This is a
  conclusion, not an omission: none of them gained a fix or an increment in
  this patch - qxribbon's only change was its CHANGELOG's section names, which
  is a record and not a fix, and the rest were not touched
- The gallery that shows the control sets is four pages, and its golden
  pictures are four now too, one per family, captured by starting the gallery
  once per style - a picture of one page cannot stand for the three it does
  not show, and a golden that mixed them would re-baseline four pages whenever
  one changed. The two application demos carry the new families and the fixes
  found by using them: the fluent pane shows what a fold and a hierarchy look
  like, wears an icon per destination so a folded pane is a column of names
  rather than one glyph repeated, and the cupertino page's alert is answered
  the way the dialog asks to be

4.7.1
---
- A patch that carries new controls, which is what a patch means in this
  project: the five files that had grown past a thousand lines are split by
  responsibility, six controls are added to the two control sets, and both
  demonstration applications are rebuilt around them. Every library's public
  API only gained declarations this time - not one signature changed, nothing
  was removed and nothing was renamed - so the compatibility promise of the 4.7
  series still holds and the project version is a patch
- qxribbon 0.14.2. ribbonbar.cpp is five files now, cut by responsibility
  rather than by size: the object model stays where it was, layout and the
  events that ask for a re-layout get a file, everything the ribbon paints
  itself gets another, the private state with its slots and the key tips get a
  third, and the two attached Qt classes get the last. No line was rewritten on
  the way and no behaviour changed; the public headers did not move at all. The
  three file-level helpers that more than one of the new files needs now live in
  ribbonbar_p.h as inline entities - that header is installed but not exported,
  so it is not part of the API
- qxdock 0.3.3. The four files of the dock are twelve now, each cut the same
  way: drag and drop, the state pair, the events and the title bar side move out
  of the file that held them, and the four private classes that used to be
  defined inside a .cpp move verbatim into dockcontainer_p.h, dockwidget_p.h and
  dockpanel_p.h. No line was rewritten and no behaviour changed, and the public
  ABI is the same one: a private class reaches its public owner through the same
  d-pointer it always did. The z-order counter stayed in the file of its only
  user, because a stateful static in a header would give every translation unit
  its own copy and the order would stop being one order
- qxfluent 0.1.1. Four controls join the set. FluentNumberBox is a number you
  can type and step, drawn rather than styled, with the value as the truth and
  the text as a way of saying it. FluentDropDownButton is a command that is a
  menu - the whole face opens it, chevron included - and FluentToggleSplitButton
  is the same shape with the other promise: a press on the left does the
  command, a press on the right opens the menu, and the button stays down while
  the command is in effect. FluentContentDialog asks a question over a dimmed
  page: the veil, the shadow and the rounded card are drawn here while the
  window underneath is still a QDialog, because modality, Escape, exec() and a
  result code are the toolkit's. Behaviour that changed in this library, each
  its own entry in its CHANGELOG: FluentNavigationView scrolls when its
  destinations do not fit instead of losing the ones past the room it has, and
  FluentCommandBar centres its row in the height the bar was given and follows
  the actions it draws, so a command that is disabled or renamed through its
  QAction now says so
- qxmaterial 0.1.1. Two controls join the set: MaterialTextField, the field that
  keeps its label, and MaterialDialog, in the two shapes M3 gives a dialog - a
  basic one that is a question with at most two answers, and a full-screen one
  that is a place to work in. MaterialTopAppBar gained actionAt(), the public
  answer to "what stands at this point" that the bar already knew privately; a
  caption can now tell a press on its icons from a press on its face, which is
  what a double click on the window and a right click on the system menu are
  made of
- qxcore 0.2.0, qxtheme 0.2.2, qxcontrols 0.1.0, qxwindow 0.3.2, qxplugin 0.3.0
  and qxapp 0.2.1 keep the numbers they had. This is a conclusion, not an
  omission: none of them was touched in this patch - qxcore and qxplugin have
  been unchanged since 4.5.0, qxtheme and qxwindow finished their own work in
  4.6.0 and 4.7.0, qxcontrols opened in 4.7.0 and nothing has reached it since,
  and what 4.7.1 changed around qxapp lives in the demos and the theme sheets
- The two demos are rebuilt around the controls rather than keeping a sample of
  each. fluentdemo opens on a caption of its own and lists every Fluents control
  as a page, with the window's pane, its settings page and its fold button
  telling each other the same thing; materialdemo is a set of whole screens -
  dashboard, album, pricing, sign in, blog, checkout and a catalogue of the
  controls - each one a template that can be copied out. Neither change reaches
  the libraries: a control that was wrong was fixed in its library, and the fix
  is in the entry above

4.7.0
---
- Three libraries join the set, and the two of them that draw controls are two
  answers to the same question rather than a skin over one: qxfluent answers it
  the way Fluent does and qxmaterial the way Material 3 does, while qxcontrols
  holds the mechanism both need and each family keeps its forms. They are
  chosen, not mixed - a control that is both is a control that is neither - and
  neither touches qxribbon: an application picks ribbon, Fluent or Material
- qxcontrols 0.1.0. New: the library opens as the base the two control sets
  draw on, reading qxtheme's token vocabularies and nothing above it. QxControl
  is what a self-drawn control builds on: it tracks where the pointer is, what
  is being pressed and whether the keyboard is on it, and answers all three as
  one state, draws the wash between the surface and the content and the focus
  ring over both, and leaves a subclass to say what its surface looks like,
  what its content is and when the pointer counts as being on it - two hooks
  rather than one because a control that drew its glyphs in the surface hook
  would have the wash land on them. QxStateLayer is that wash: default
  opacities per state, layered() as a value a test can predict, paint() as the
  draw, and the colour left to the family. QxRingPainter draws the ring a focus
  or a selection is made of as the band between two rounded rectangles rather
  than a stroke along a midline, which would put half of it outside the band
  and leave the corners uneven; a negative inset is the ring hanging outside
  the control, which is how Fluent draws its outer one. QxShadowPainter turns
  an elevation tier into pixels: a tile rendered once for a set of parameters
  and stretched over the rectangle in nine pieces, cached so the second card of
  the same shape costs a lookup; the blur is sixteen concentric rings rather
  than a true gaussian, which reads as one smooth falloff at shadow sizes and
  keeps the render deterministic. QxControlAccessible is the face a control
  that paints itself hands an assistive tool - a role and a state cannot carry
  a slider's number, so a control can supply and own one of its own
- qxfluent 0.1.0. New: the library opens with the scales its controls are drawn
  from - a six-step corner ladder that is deliberately not the sheets' five, a
  type ramp, an elevation scale and a duration scale - and with the controls
  themselves. FluentSwitch is a pill with a knob and a sentence beside it, on a
  Fluent duration that reduce-motion and the theme's scale reach without the
  control asking about either. FluentInfoBar is a message that stays until it
  is dealt with, coloured by its severity because a theme has a role for a
  highlight and none for "warning". FluentExpander is a header that keeps a
  body and opens to show it, the header being the control and the body
  travelling out from under it. FluentRating is a row of stars that shows what
  a click would do before it is clicked. FluentProgressRing says how far along
  something is, or only that something is happening. FluentSlider fills its
  rail with the accent and divides it with a mark at every interval.
  FluentCard is a surface whose lift is what says it sits above the page.
  FluentBadge is the application pointing at something, in the accent.
  FluentReveal is the wash that follows the pointer over a list.
  FluentCommandBar is where a Fluent application's commands live,
  FluentBreadcrumbBar is the path back, and FluentTeachingTip is a flyout that
  points at the thing it is about. FluentNavigationView and FluentTabView are
  the skeletons an application is navigated in and several things are open at
  once in. FluentWindowMaterial is what a Fluent window is
  made of - Mica, Mica Alt and Acrylic - and it is the one place this library
  reaches qxwindow, because a material is the host's to draw
- qxmaterial 0.1.0. New: the library opens with its own scales - corner, type,
  elevation and a duration-and-easing set - and with the controls drawn from
  them. MaterialSwitch fills its track with the primary colour and crosses it
  with a handle that carries a mark once it is on, so its state is read off the
  handle alone. MaterialSlider says nothing about intervals, because the
  handle's position is the answer and a second one would answer it again.
  MaterialBadge is the error colour and stops counting past its maximum,
  because "127" is not more useful than "99+" and it is wider.
  MaterialSegmentedButton is a row of choices divided into equal parts, where
  the part in force is the one that is filled. MaterialChip stands for one
  choice or does one thing. MaterialCard holds something, in one of three
  weights. MaterialFAB is the one thing a screen is for.
  MaterialProgressRing, MaterialSnackbar and MaterialSkeleton are what says
  something is happening, what just happened and what has not arrived yet.
  MaterialRipple is the answer a surface gives when it is pressed, an event
  filter on any widget rather than a control of its own.
  MaterialNavigationRail is the column an application is navigated in - fixed
  width, its items in a column centred in the width it really has - and
  MaterialTopAppBar is the top of a screen with three places in one bar
- qxtheme 0.2.1 -> 0.2.2. New: QxThemeInfo::density and motionScale, with
  defaults that leave every existing registration compiling and behaving as it
  did - the density tier a control library draws at (Comfortable) and the
  multiplier the application's motion reads through (1.0), installed by apply()
  the way the palette is. qxthememotion.h is the motion vocabulary a transition
  reads its answer from: three tiers of duration and curve named by what the
  movement is for - a hover, a change with distance to cover, an entrance with
  a settle - and motionFor() honours the reduce-motion switch by turning every
  tier into a jump to the end state, so compliance is the default rather than
  something each control remembers to do. QxThemeManager::setReduceMotion() is
  that switch, persisted under the ui group and announced through
  reduceMotionChanged(). qxthemeelevation.h is the shadow vocabulary a painted
  shadow reads: five tiers from a surface that casts nothing to one that floats
  over everything, each a blur radius, a y offset and an alpha over the theme's
  shadow colour. QxThemeManager::additionalStyleSheet() puts the application's
  own sheet after the theme's instead of replacing it - an application-level
  sheet is one string, so an application that had one competed with this engine
  for it and whichever ran last won - and the text is the application's: not
  persisted, not part of a theme, and not installed while no theme is in force
- qxribbon 0.14.0 -> 0.14.1. Fixed: the quick access bar takes the width it asks
  for once a style sheet has reached it, including a sheet installed after the
  window was already on the screen. What the bar's layout gives it is the width
  of its size hint, and a sheet decides what that hint is, so a sheet that
  arrived late changed the hint after the only pass that would have answered it
  had run and the last button was cut off by the widget that followed it. The
  request the tool bar posts when its own size changes is now a reason to lay
  the bar out again, next to the one the corner widgets already had
- qxdock 0.3.1 -> 0.3.2. Cleanup: the fall-back sheet resolves the theme's
  corner tiers the way the generator resolves them for a generated sheet - a
  radius in base.css names a tier instead of a pixel count, and the renderer
  substitutes the tier's value, the same value tools/themes keeps. An
  application that loads a generated theme is unaffected; an application that
  writes its own fragment with pixel values keeps working, because only
  {{...}} is substituted
- qxcore 0.2.0, qxwindow 0.3.2, qxplugin 0.3.0 and qxapp 0.2.1: unchanged. All
  four were settled before this cycle and nothing in 4.7 repaired or added to
  any of them - no number moved (K11). This is a project minor with no library
  minor in it, and that is not an oversight: the whole cycle added 40 public
  headers, modified 2 with declarations and nothing else, and deleted or
  renamed none, so no library had an interface change to declare
- themes: the sheets gain the vocabularies the controls read rather than more
  colours. corner-{none,sm,md,lg,pill} (0/2/3/6/9999px) and
  type-{caption,subtitle,body,title} (10/13/15/18px) name values the sheets
  already carried, space-{xs,sm,md,lg} (4/8/12/16px) puts the five chrome
  fragments' tab padding on a 4px grid - flat, slate and soft land byte for
  byte, while classic and accent were at 10px and snap to 8 and 12, which is a
  visible +/-2px on the ribbon's tab padding and the one look this release
  changes. The generator refuses to write a sheet that still carries a bare
  border-radius or a numeric font size, so the vocabulary cannot quietly
  decay; the token names are unchanged and only widened, so nothing written
  against the old ones breaks
- demos and examples: controlgallery is one window that shows both control
  sets and switches between them, fluentdemo and materialdemo are two small
  applications built the way each design language says an application is built
  - a navigation column, a top bar and a page of controls - and the two of them
  share the gallery's widgets rather than re-implementing them
- ci and build: every build leg now gives --parallel a number, because a bare
  -j is not "one job per core" on a Makefile generator and the runners had been
  asked for an unbounded one; the Windows Qt 6.12 leg is out until Qt can be
  installed on that runner, which leaves the six legs of 4.6 as five for now
- docs: every guide page was read against the tree after 4.7 landed, which is
  how three new libraries came to be missing from the front page's component
  table, the four skeleton controls from the component list, and the theme's
  whole 4.7 half from the theming page - the patch ledger records what was
  missing and the two lessons it taught. A 4.6 note is corrected in passing:
  equally specific rules are decided by the later declaration, not the earlier
  one, measured on Qt 5.15.2 and 6.8.3 both
- Note: this release breaks nothing. doc/pages/migration.md, section "4.7",
  lists what an application may want to look at - new fields on QxThemeInfo
  whose defaults keep old registrations compiling, the two token vocabularies
  that were widened and the two families whose ribbon tab padding moved by 2px,
  and the two entry points - the reduce-motion switch and the additional style
  sheet - that are switches rather than breaks. The three new libraries are
  0.x and carry no compatibility promise of their own yet

4.6.0
---
- The libraries stop naming colours inside their own style sheets, and take them
  from the theme instead. A sheet that says `#f5f5f5` is a sheet that only works
  on one window; qxdock's declarations now carry placeholders that a
  DockStyleColors fills - where a colour sits in the dock, and separately what
  goes on top of the accent - and qxtheme's themes publish an accent as a pair,
  so a glyph on the accent fill finds the colour that reads on it. The dock's
  rules moved into the themes themselves, one sheet with the ribbon's, so
  applicationStyleSheet() no longer joins two renderings and no longer loses to
  its own fall-back. Icons follow: a single-colour glyph is repainted from the
  palette the theme installed, from six entry points in qxtheme, and every
  library that ships glyphs re-asks when the palette changes
- qxtheme 0.2.0 -> 0.2.1. New: QxThemeInfo::accent, installed as
  QPalette::Highlight with HighlightedText as its pair, and accentColor()
  answering with the theme's accent before the platform's; applyColorScheme(bool)
  and followSystemColorScheme(), which tell the platform which way the theme
  paints - on Windows 11 the windows11 style draws from its own table chosen by
  the system scheme and never reads the palette, so a light theme on a dark
  desktop came out with dark input fields, dark scroll bars and a dark title bar,
  and setting a light palette could not reach any of it; and tintedPixmap(),
  tintedIcon(), themedPixmap() and themedIcon() in qxthemeicon.h, which rasterise
  a glyph and repaint it through its alpha so the colour the file was drawn in is
  replaced outright and the antialiased edges keep their coverage. Fixed: a
  restored theme waited for its registration instead of being rejected against an
  engine that had not read it yet, a theme now states the colour that reads on
  its own fill rather than letting the palette compute a different answer (a
  saturated blue was being paired with black while the sheet paired it with
  white), and the themed icons are repainted when the palette changes
- qxwindow 0.3.1 -> 0.3.2. New: WindowAgentBase::setBorderOwnsCursor() takes the
  resize border out of the hit test. A frameless window draws its own border and
  the border it draws is not always there to be grabbed - it may be no more than
  the room a shadow needs, with dragging meant to go through the title bar only -
  and nothing in the geometry says which of the two it is. Default true, which is
  the behaviour every window has always had; this is the opt-out whose absence
  0.3.0 recorded as a limitation. Both backends honour it, and the answer differs
  either side of the window edge, because there is nothing outside it to hand a
  press to. Cleanup: the Qt 5.14 guard around the private qwinregistry_p.h
  include is gone, the header being below the 5.15 minimum
- qxdock 0.3.0 -> 0.3.1. New: DockStyleColors and the accent-soft token it fills -
  the wash a button hovers with, enough accent to read as emphasis and enough
  surface to stay a hover; dockStyleSheet(const DockStyleColors &, bool)
  substitutes the whole set, dockStyleSheet(bool) stays and renders with
  fromPalette() of the application palette, and a field left invalid falls back
  per field while an unresolved placeholder refuses the sheet and says so rather
  than letting Qt drop the declaration silently. The dock's icon buttons state
  their own hover instead of taking the platform's pale rounded wash, which read
  as a shape pasted on the bar; the glyphs say which of two surfaces they are on
  rather than which colours to use, so the glyph on the accent fill is painted in
  HighlightedText; and dockPlatformStyleSheet() hands the platform's piece out on
  its own. Fixed: an auto hide bar's accent no longer loses to what sits on it; a
  hover glyph is tinted like the resting one and the two fills are separated; the
  drop indicator takes the theme it is shown in - it cached the first valid colour
  it computed and painted its bitmap once, so a window that switched theme kept a
  five-way indicator in the old palette until it was rebuilt; and the icon buttons
  have a height both schemes share, the dark skeleton's min-height on ordinary
  buttons having reached the layout through the title bar and the tab close
  button
- qxribbon 0.13.0 -> 0.14.0. Interface: RibbonGalleryGroup paints its own items
  instead of being a QListView, and the class it inherits is now QWidget. The view
  was carrying machinery the gallery never used well - a model per group, a
  delegate, a selection model, and a mirror group inside the gallery fed from the
  current group's model, so the strip showed a copy, the popup held the original
  and a click was forwarded between them. The item states were left to the sheet,
  and the sheet could not deliver them: an ::item rule on a custom class matched
  nothing, so hover and selection were whatever the platform style had left
  behind, and the one pseudo-state that did land moved the item's contents by a
  pixel, which is the jumping. A state now changes the wash and the border and
  never the geometry, the label keeps QPalette::Text instead of reversing to
  white on a wash, and the popup paints each group with the same painter, so the
  strip and the popup cannot disagree about where an item is. What the view leaked
  is gone - no model(), currentIndex(), scrollTo() or QListView's spacing() - and
  the group says what it now is: itemCount(), itemAction(), selectedIndex(),
  itemRect(), setSpacing(), gridSize(), and the viewport/scroll triple the gallery
  drives; currentViewGroup() returns the group itself rather than a copy.
  Addition: RibbonPageContext::addPage(RibbonPage *) takes a page the caller
  built, RibbonBar::setCornerWidget(QWidget *, Qt::Corner) is the ribbon-aware way
  to reach the bar's top corners, and a zh_CN catalogue sits next to the sources
  with every string the library shows translated in it. Fixed: the gallery's last
  row ends inside the strip instead of running past its bottom edge; the popup
  keeps one item size whatever the strip is showing and stays on screen, its
  height measured at the width it really gets rather than at a guessed one; two
  strings lupdate could not see were in a context the runtime never looked up, so
  their translations could not be found; the ScreenTip repaints from the palette
  on every show; the minimize button is painted from the palette; the application
  button is laid out on the tab strip's line and takes the theme when it is a
  caller's own; the quick access menu triangle is gone; a page can be taken into a
  context that already exists; and the close glyph no longer borrows the
  highlight's pair
- qxapp 0.2.0 -> 0.2.1. New: ThemeInstaller keeps the whole theme in force on the
  application - the sheet, and the dock's icons tinted from the palette it
  installed - because a theme switch is a signal while the platform swapping its
  own light or dark palette arrives as an application event with no signal, and an
  application that wires up the first and forgets the second looks right until the
  desktop changes; applicationStyleSheet() hands out the theme as one application
  sheet and appends only the dock's platform piece, the rules that belong to one
  desktop and are empty off X11; and readBundledTheme() reads the two colours a
  sheet cannot carry from res/themes/accents.json, keyed by theme id rather than
  one file per theme
- qxcore 0.2.0 and qxplugin 0.3.0: unchanged. Both were settled at 4.5.0 and
  nothing in this cycle touched them - no repair, no addition - so neither number
  moved (K11). What 4.6 touched in their neighbourhood is the catalogue the
  libraries ship, and a catalogue is a file next to the sources, not an interface
- demos and examples: ribbondemo is rebuilt around themed icons - Tabler Icons
  3.49.0 under MIT, the licence text next to them, the old sets removed and every
  page one file - and starts under a named theme, takes `--theme a,b` for a
  sequence and `--lang` for its catalogue; dockdemo takes the theme from the
  shared installer, paints the dock from the theme's colour roles, accepts
  `--theme a,b` and answers `--inspect` with the tree it is actually showing;
  and every sample in examples/ that shows a ribbon, a dock or the shell now
  installs a bundled theme, defaulting to soft-light
- i18n: qxribbon, qxdock and qxapp each ship a zh_CN catalogue built and installed
  with the library - 116 strings translated, nine diagnostics deliberately left
  in English so the same words can be searched whatever language the interface
  runs in - and one test holds the three catalogues together. The new
  internationalisation page says how an application reaches them, and adding a
  language is a `LANGUAGES` entry in three CMakeLists.txt plus the translations
- ci and build: Qt 6.12 now gates on Windows too, six legs in all; the icon policy
  - one drawing style, sources and licences recorded - is checked in the format
  job; and the sources are held to the UTF-8 the build assumes
- docs: an icon policy and an internationalisation page, a theming page that says
  which half of an application's colours belongs to the platform, a contributing
  guide that lets the change decide how much verification a commit needs, and a
  release of the 4.6 task ledger recording what was measured against what was
  assumed - including the dock's theme switch, which turned out to keep a metric
  the old theme set rather than a colour
- Note: this release breaks source and binary compatibility in one place -
  RibbonGalleryGroup no longer inherits QListView. doc/pages/migration.md, section
  "4.6", lists what an application has to touch

4.5.0
---
- The libraries stop carrying a look of their own, and the themes are rebuilt.
  qxribbon's bundled style sheets and its RibbonTheme went away, qxdock's four
  went with them, and both now hand the sheets out for the application to build
  in and apply: a widget-level sheet outranks the application-level one, which is
  why an application that asked for a dark theme kept a light ribbon and a light
  DockWindow. res/themes/ is rebuilt around that - five families (soft, classic,
  flat, slate and accent), each light and dark, generated by
  tools/themes/build_themes.py from one source per family, and the generator now
  refuses a palette whose text does not clear a contrast floor over its own
  background or whose selected, hovered and disabled states cannot be told apart.
  qxapp registers the ten in one call, registerBundledThemes(), and reads one
  with readBundledTheme()
- qxtheme 0.1.1 -> 0.2.0. The engine addresses a theme by id and registers none
  of its own: the Theme enumeration is gone with themeId(), themeName(),
  themeFromId(), isDarkTheme(), themeStyleSheetPath() and themeStyleSheet(), and
  with them the table that mirrored the ribbon's own enumeration. An application
  hands registerTheme() a QxThemeInfo - an id, a name, whether the theme is dark,
  the style sheet - and picks one with setTheme(id); theme(),
  systemLightTheme() and systemDarkTheme() answer in ids and themeChanged()
  carries one. palette(Theme) becomes paletteFor(bool dark), setStyleSheetFile()
  is gone and setStyleSheet() takes an id. Ids were always what was stored, so a
  theme written by an earlier version still restores. Added: systemAccentColor(),
  and QxThemeManager::accentColor()/setAccentColor() with accentColorChanged() -
  the platform's colour is read once, before the first apply(), and only an
  override reaches the palette, because a theme carries a highlight of its own.
  A minor, because the interface moved
- qxribbon 0.12.2 -> 0.13.0. New: RibbonMdiArea, a tabbed-document area that
  swaps the ribbon's pages with the active document; RibbonBackstageView, the
  full-takeover panel the application button opens; RibbonStatusBar and
  RibbonSliderPane; Alt KeyTips that cascade from the tabs and the application
  button down into a page's buttons; a rich ScreenTip on hover; a context menu on
  the bar; and a reduced presentation a group falls back to before the page
  reaches for its scroll buttons. Accessibility: pages and groups mirror their
  title into accessibleName and a QAccessible factory gives the painted
  containers the Pane and Grouping roles. Interface: RibbonBar::RibbonStyle names
  its four values for the layout each one selects rather than for the product it
  was modelled on - OfficeStyle is TabBarBelowStyle and so on - and
  isOfficeStyle() is hasOwnTabRow(), which is the question it always answered;
  RibbonTheme, setRibbonTheme(), ribbonTheme() and loadTheme() are gone; the
  ribbon's own glyphs are alpha masks painted in a palette role, so one asset
  serves light and dark and a theme reaches them through the palette alone.
  Fixed: the tab strip re-measures itself after a style change instead of keeping
  the previous theme's width and growing a pair of scroll arrows; the placeholder
  tab a minimized ribbon selects costs no width; the ScreenTip and the backstage
  arrow read the palette instead of a hard-coded light sheet; and
  RibbonManager::setupFactory() never leaves the manager without a factory, which
  passing null used to do to the next element the library created
- qxdock 0.2.3 -> 0.3.0. New: saveStateToFile() and restoreStateFromFile(), a
  layout as plain XML a person can open, hand-edit or diff rather than the
  compressed blob saveState() returns; DockDrag, which carries a dock widget over
  a native drag and lets a window accept one - docking moved widgets with plain
  mouse events, so nothing could ever arrive from another process; an auto hide
  container that slides in and out behind DockManager::AutoHideAnimationEnabled,
  off by default because a container that slides is a visual change and nothing
  verifies looks here; and accessible names and roles for the painted containers.
  DockWindow no longer applies a style sheet to itself - the four sheets moved
  out and qxdock::dockStyleSheet() hands them to the application - and the
  built-in icons can be tinted to the palette with qxdock::applyDockIcons() and
  refreshDockIcons() instead of shipping a hard-coded black. Fixed: an auto hide
  overlay jumped into place, because every frame of its slide posted a resize the
  container's own event filter answered by stopping the slide
- qxplugin 0.2.1 -> 0.3.0. Loading is cut in two: resolvePlugins() reads the
  metadata, resolves the graph and computes the order without starting anything,
  and requestPlugin(id) starts one plugin together with what it needs - so a host
  can show its window before paying for its modules. The graph is queryable too,
  with dependenciesOf(), dependentsOf() and missingDependenciesOf() answering
  from the metadata, which is what a window that lets someone switch a plugin off
  has to ask before anything is loaded
- qxcore 0.1.3 -> 0.2.0. QxTranslator::translate() looks a string up in the
  catalogues this object installed - the class said in its own documentation that
  a slot may call translate() and then never offered it - and QxSettings reads
  and writes a whole group in one step with section() and setSection(), which is
  the one thing a restore path wants and beginGroup()/endGroup() leave to be
  paired by hand. childGroups() and childKeys() join allKeys(). setVersion() was
  the one write that reached QSettings without a word and now reports through
  valueChanged() like every other
- qxapp 0.1.2 -> 0.2.0. QxNavBar is renamed QxNavTabBar: it is a QxTabBar-derived
  tab bar sitting one letter from QxNavigationBar, the shell's vertical rail,
  with which it shares neither a base nor a purpose. Breaking, and no
  compatibility alias is kept, because the point of the rename is to stop the two
  from reading as variants of one another. QxAppShell gained addLayoutGroup() and
  createStandardShell(), the scaffolding every ribbon shell had been assembling
  by hand, and QxToast is announced as a Notification rather than as a bare
  widget. Fixed: RibbonAppWindow lays its title bar out before
  updateWindowFlags() returns, which is the frame the limitations page recorded
  as the button group overlapping for a moment
- qxwindow 0.3.1: unchanged. Its only change this cycle is a version guard below
  the 5.15 minimum, and build wiring does not move a number (K11) - the entry
  stays in the library's own CHANGELOG
- demos and examples: ribbondemo and dockdemo show the new features - the
  backstage panel, the status bar, the MDI area, the XML layout file and the
  native drag among them - and examples/ gained samples of its own for the same,
  including Example_Ribbon_Backstage, Example_Ribbon_MdiTabs and the
  standard-shell convenience
- ci and build: the sources are held to the UTF-8 the build assumes, by a check
  that strips Qt's own -utf-8 and so proves the tree's switch is what makes a
  Chinese literal survive; the macOS leg builds qxdock next to qxwindow and
  leaves the demos out; MSVC is told the sources are UTF-8; and the qt5 and qt6
  presets moved to Ninja
- docs: a contributing guide, the build, architecture, ci and doxygen pages
  promoted out of the private notes, a version table of its own, and a licence
  header on every file that lacked one
- Note: this release breaks source compatibility in several places at once - the
  theme ids, qxtheme's interface, the ribbon style names, QxNavBar - because 4.5
  is the minor in which API may change (K26). doc/pages/migration.md, section
  "4.5", is the list of what an application has to touch

4.4.2
---
- ci: Qt 6.12 LTS now gates, on Linux, next to Qt 6.8.1 on all three platforms
  and Qt 5.15.2 on Linux. It was added report only first - nobody had compiled
  this tree against 6.12, so the first run could have been red - and it was:
  Qt 6.12 does not install the QPA headers, so qxdock's X11 helper, which asked
  QPlatformNativeInterface for the connection by name, stopped compiling. It asks
  QNativeInterface::QX11Application instead, which is public and typed and reaches
  the same connection; Qt 5 keeps the old call. The second 6.12 failure never
  appeared on that leg: 6.12 ships the private Qt modules as packages of their
  own, so qxwindow's Qt5::CorePrivate no longer arrived with Qt6Core, and the root
  project now asks for CorePrivate and GuiPrivate explicitly. That failure is
  Windows-only - the leg is Linux, where the native Windows backend is not
  configured in - so a green 6.12 leg still does not mean 6.12 builds on Windows;
  a local 6.12 is what found it
- qxcore: a log rotation that could not reopen its file after moving it away said
  nothing and dropped every line after it, the reopen's result having been
  discarded. The result is now checked and the failure reported on stderr, the one
  channel left to a logger that cannot call qWarning() without re-entering itself
- qxdock: three fixes. loadStylesheet() dropped the result of opening the
  compiled-in stylesheet and, if the open failed, went on to set the empty string
  it had read - an empty stylesheet being how an application's own stylesheet gets
  wiped - and now leaves the stylesheet alone and says why.
  DockPanel::minimumSizeHint() asked whether the title bar was visible where the
  question is whether it is hidden, and before a window is shown the whole tree
  answers false, so the hint came out a title bar short. And
  QApplication::setActiveWindow(), deprecated as of 6.12, is now
  QWidget::activateWindow()
- qxribbon: RibbonPage clamps its offset after measuring instead of before, so
  widening a page that had been scrolled to the right no longer leaves a gap along
  the right edge until the next click (R6); and the window button group is placed
  while the window is still hidden, which is what had been leaving it on the left
- qxapp: the navigation rail's entries stretch across the rail instead of keeping
  their own width
- demos and tests: the version guards below the 5.15 minimum are gone, which is
  what two earlier sweeps had left behind - one swept the "<" direction in
  src/libs, the other the ">=" direction in demos, and the "<" direction in demos
  and examples had never been swept at all. DockDemo gained the --screenshot
  switch and a golden image, so qxdock, which has no demo of its own, is covered
  by visual regression after all
- qxcore 0.1.2 -> 0.1.3. The log rotation fix above. Patch, per appendix B:
  a patch is "an addition or a fix", and no exported symbol moved
- qxribbon 0.12.1 -> 0.12.2. Two fixes: the page offset clamp and the window
  button group. The dead Qt version branches this release also dropped in
  `ribbonbar`, `ribboncontainers` and `ribboncustomizewidget` are build wiring
  and are not what moves the number
- qxdock 0.2.2 -> 0.2.3. Three fixes: the stylesheet, the minimum size hint and
  the activation call. The X11 helper's new route to the connection is the same
  connection by another question - it is part of this step because the library
  changed, not because it is a defect
- qxapp 0.1.1 -> 0.1.2. The navigation rail fix
- qxtheme 0.1.1, qxwindow 0.3.1, qxplugin 0.2.1: unchanged. `qxtheme` and
  `qxwindow` each lost one Qt version guard below the 5.15 minimum and nothing
  else, which is build wiring - appendix B's own example is deleting
  `header.list`, which was recorded and did not move any version either - and
  `qxplugin` received no change at all. The four libraries that did move carry
  their `Unreleased` section over as the new version, in the same commit

4.4.1
---
- examples: the eleven qxapp classes that had a unit test but no visual sample now
  have one, as four family samples rather than one sample per class - a family is
  what a user looks at, and eleven windows over the same four ideas would have
  hidden that. Example_App_PropertyEditor covers QxProperty, QxPropertyEditor and
  QxSettingsDialog against one file-backed QxSettings, so an edit made in the form
  is the edit the dialog shows; Example_App_Buttons covers QxToolButton,
  QxMenuButton, QxMenuAccessButton and QxExtensionButton; Example_App_NavigationBar
  covers QxNavigationBar (the vertical rail, driving a page stack) and QxNavBar (a
  QxTabBar subclass with a customize menu) - different base classes behind similar
  names; Example_App_TabWidget covers QxTabWidget and QxTabBar. doc/pages/build.md
  example count 23 -> 27
- tests: one in-process smoke test per new sample. Each compiles the sample's own
  mainwindow.cpp, stands the window up offscreen and looks up the classes the sample
  exists for, so a sample cannot rot silently - if its source stops compiling or its
  window starts crashing, the test fails. Launching the sample executables through
  QProcess was tried first and does not work here: CreateProcess fails for them on
  Windows whatever the environment or working directory is, while the same
  executables start fine when launched by hand. The files live in
  tests/qxapp/examples/ because tests/qxapp/auto/ is swept by a tst_*.cpp glob that
  would have registered each of them a second time
- qxcore 0.1.2, qxtheme 0.1.1, qxwindow 0.3.1, qxribbon 0.12.1, qxdock 0.2.2,
  qxplugin 0.2.1, qxapp 0.1.1: unchanged. 4.4.1 touched examples, tests and docs
  only - src/libs has no change at all since the 4.4.0 tag - so no library version
  moves (K11)

4.4.0
---
- qxwindow 0.3.0 -> 0.3.1. Internal cleanup only - no public API changed, so the
  step is a patch. The frameless hit-test now asks the system buttons and the
  hit-test-visible items the same question from one place, and the private log
  macros are renamed QWK_* -> QXWINDOW_* with the category becoming "qxwindow"
  (was "qwindowkit"): set QT_LOGGING_RULES=qxwindow.*=true in place of
  qwindowkit.*. The window-context refactor is private to windowcontext_p.h and is
  unchanged in effect
- qxribbon 0.12.0 -> 0.12.1. Three bug fixes, none touching the exported surface,
  so a patch. RibbonCustomizeWidget recorded a "move down" on an action as a
  "move up" (the action branch handed -1 to makeChangeActionOrderCustomizeData,
  where -1 means up); the ribbon bar named its tab bar twice so the stacked widget
  was left unnamed; RibbonPage left two private fields uninitialised and a wheel
  event or scroll-button click before the first layout compared an indeterminate
  value. RibbonCustomizeWidget::isChanged() and model() are removed in this release
  - the class carries no export macro, so per the project red line ("not exported =>
  not API") that is not an API change and the version does not move on it
- qxdock 0.2.1 -> 0.2.2. Three bug fixes, patch. DockWindow left its globally locked
  features uninitialised, so every dock widget in a window reported a feature set
  with arbitrary bits removed; hideDockWidget() deleted the content of a
  DeleteContentOnClose widget that had been taken back on purpose and dereferenced a
  null pointer; pinning an already-pinned widget crashed because
  DockWidget::setAutoHide() / DockPanel::setAutoHide() only substituted a real side
  on the path that creates the auto-hide container, not on the path that moves an
  already-pinned widget (R7, R8, R12 in the 4.4 plan)
- qxcore 0.1.2, qxtheme 0.1.1, qxplugin 0.2.1, qxapp 0.1.1: unchanged. None of the
  four received a code change in 4.4 - every change in the tree was in tests,
  demos, or docs - so their versions stay where 4.3.0 left them. The version number
  is the library's statement of "did the interface move"; with no interface move it
  does not move (K11)
- tests: qxapp's seven leaf controls - QxTabBar, QxTabWidget, QxNavBar,
  QxToolButton, QxMenuButton, QxMenuAccessButton, QxExtensionButton - had no unit
  test at all and now have one each (tst_qxappwidgets), offscreen-only minimal
  assertions. The four property/settings classes were already covered. This is the
  curated half of the qxapp example gap; the visual samples land in 4.4.1
- demos: the qxwindow demo is back. It went away with the qmake-only demos and was
  rebuilt on the current API (setup() + setTitleBar(), the four system buttons with
  WindowIcon, the full set of window materials, showSystemMenu() on the icon button)
  rather than restored - the original leaned on a WindowStyleAgent and a
  WindowBar/WindowButton pair that were deleted in the same commit. It links qxwindow
  and nothing else
- docs: doc/pages/build.md lists the demo again and the example count is 23; the
  qxapp example modules that still lack a visual sample are noted for 4.4.1

4.3.0
---
- qxribbon 0.11.0 -> 0.12.0. FramelessHelper and the window class on top of it,
  RibbonWindow, are gone. Both were deprecated in 4.2 (K27 / K26) and the K26
  buffer - a removal waits at least one release - has now run out.
  FramelessHelper was the frameless backend qxribbon carried so that it needed no
  dependency on qxwindow; only its Windows + Qt 5 path was complete, and
  everywhere else a window first shown maximised could not be restored.
  RibbonWindow was RibbonMainWindow plus that backend and nothing else, so the
  two went together rather than leaving an empty shell behind. On every Qt
  version the answer is QxApp::RibbonAppWindow, which frames the same
  RibbonMainWindow through qxwindow's native path; on Qt 5 that costs a
  dependency on qxapp, and qxwindow's native backend does build there - checked
  before deleting, and the reason this did not wait for Qt 5 to be dropped.
  Every sample moves to RibbonMainWindow. RibbonGalleryViewport also loses its
  export macro: it lives in a private header, was privatized on purpose, and no
  caller names it. See doc/pages/migration.md, section "4.3 已删除"
- qxwindow 0.2.0 -> 0.3.0. WindowStyleAgent is gone (deprecated in 4.2 under
  K26), and so is the QX_WINDOW_CONFIG_STYLE_AGENT option that compiled it.
  HighContrast was the one value QxThemeManager does not report, and it had no
  consumer anywhere in the tree - checked before deleting, and the reason the
  class does not come back in a smaller "just HighContrast" form. The public
  surface is now two classes, and it was walked end to end: the only consumer in
  the tree is qxapp's RibbonAppWindow with six members, and the three members
  that had no test at all - centralize(), raise() and showSystemMenu() - have
  one now
- qxcore 0.1.1 -> 0.1.2, qxtheme 0.1.0 -> 0.1.1, qxdock 0.2.0 -> 0.2.1,
  qxplugin 0.2.0 -> 0.2.1, qxapp 0.1.0 -> 0.1.1. All five move by patch for the
  same one-line reason: QX_<LIB>_DEPRECATED_SINCE never answered true for a
  deprecation that had already happened, because
  QX_<LIB>_DISABLE_DEPRECATED_BEFORE defaulted to the library's own version and
  (0,11,0) > (0,11,0) is false. All seven libraries defined the macro and not
  one used it, so the mechanism was dead and a deprecation silently lasted
  forever. The default is now a fixed floor, and tst_qxdeprecated covers all
  seven libraries
- tests: qxdock had 8 exported classes that no test mentioned and qxribbon had
  12; both are covered now, 32 and 52 cases. Two bugs surfaced while writing
  them and are fixed: RibbonTheme::setTheme(RibbonTheme::CustomTheme)
  dereferenced the widget argument, whose documented default is a null pointer,
  and RibbonCtrlContainer built vertical centred its icon while one switched
  vertical afterwards left-aligned it
- qxwindow: the "frameless window cannot be maximised on first show" entry on
  the limitations page is no longer something only a person can notice. A probe
  reproduces it on a plain Qt window with no qtcanpool in it and is recorded as
  an expected failure on Qt 6 + Windows - measured as reproducing on 6.8.3 and
  passing on 5.15.2 - so the day the Qt plugin is fixed the case reports an
  unexpected pass instead of staying quiet. A second case guards the half the
  library controls: its own window must not ask Qt for a frameless window. That
  one holds offscreen and runs on every CI leg; the probe needs a window manager
  and skips there
- Qt 5: still supported and still built, and this release deletes none of it,
  but what a removal would mean is written down now. doc/pages/migration.md says
  who a removal in 5.0.0 affects - the library's own usage does not change,
  find_package and the QtCanpool:: targets are identical on Qt 6, while the
  caller's own Qt 5 usage does - what to do with the two qtcompat helpers, and
  that the thirty < 6.0 branches are mostly compile-time adaptations with a few
  that follow semantic changes in Qt itself. There is no date: K32 sets three
  trigger conditions instead
- docs: doc/pages/limitations.md records that CI does not cover Qt 6.12 (K31).
  That entry is deliberately marked as a boundary of the evidence rather than a
  known bad behaviour, and it says when it can be deleted; every "on Qt 6" claim
  on that page is evidence up to 6.8 only
- examples: the samples were checked against this release rather than changed to
  have something to show, because nothing in A1-A7 changed behaviour - A1
  removes a constant-false branch, A2 is an extraction, A3/A4/A6/A7 are tests
  and A5 is an assessment. One stale thing was found and fixed: a commented-out
  setRibbonTheme(RibbonTheme::WpsdarkTheme) in examples/qxribbon/simple named an
  enumerator that does not exist

4.2.0
---
- qxribbon 0.10.2 -> 0.11.0. FramelessHelper is deprecated (K27). It is the
  frameless backend of RibbonWindow and only really works on Windows with Qt 5;
  everywhere else - every Qt 6 platform included - it takes the incomplete half
  of the path, where a window first shown maximised cannot be restored. Use
  QxApp::RibbonAppWindow instead, which goes through qxwindow. Removal waits at
  least one more release under the K26 buffer; RibbonWindow itself is unchanged.
  RibbonStyleOption also gained setBaseHeight(), setTitleBarHeight() and
  setTabBarHeight(), so the ribbon size layout can be tuned without subclassing
- qxwindow 0.1.2 -> 0.2.0. WindowStyleAgent is deprecated (K26 / W2): nothing in
  the tree refers to it, no page documents it, it is not even compiled unless
  QX_WINDOW_CONFIG_STYLE_AGENT is on, and QxThemeManager in qxtheme already
  follows the system colour scheme more completely than this class does. The
  resize border geometry also moved out of windowcontext_qt.cpp into
  windowgeometry_p.h as plain functions over a QSize, so that arithmetic is
  covered by tests for the first time
- qxdock stays at 0.2.0. The 0.2.0 "perspective and group" surface - DockGroup,
  DockGroupManager and DockSubWindow - had neither a dedicated example nor any
  test until now; both were added (tst_DockGroup, examples/qxdock/dockgroup).
  Its single header change this cycle, dockautohidecontainer.h, is only the
  comment A13 added, which is not an API change under appendix B, so the number
  does not move; what changed is recorded in the library's own Unreleased
  section instead (K24)
- qxcore, qxtheme, qxplugin and qxapp do not move (0.1.1 / 0.1.0 / 0.2.0 /
  0.1.0): no public header of theirs changed at all this cycle. These four had
  no library-level changelog before; they do now (K24, non-retroactive)
- debt: every TODO and FIXME in src/libs and in the rest of the tracked tree is
  settled through one of K21's three exits - fixed with a regression test,
  rewritten as a NOTE pointing at an entry in doc/pages/limitations.md, or
  deleted with the reason written down
- examples: all 22 targets under examples/ now carry a one-line README, and
  Example_Dock_Group is new
- docs: doc/pages/release.md is new (K28) and holds every step of cutting a
  release in one page; the component dependencies and class names in index.md,
  architecture.md and components.md were corrected to match the code
- build and CI: projects/template is now rendered by scripts/new-project and
  compared byte for byte in CI; thirdparty/ and the redundant
  scripts/project.py are gone; the AUTOMOC workaround qtc_autogen_rescan() was
  re-verified on CMake 4.2.1 against both Qt 5 and Qt 6 and is still required
  (C3); the screenshot golden is now kept per Qt major version, because Qt 5 and
  Qt 6 do not produce the same window size and a shared golden made Qt 5 fail
  every time (C2)
- X11: qxdock's linux/ subtree does get compiled on the Ubuntu CI legs, but
  every test runs offscreen, so is_platform_x11() short-circuits each request:
  the xcb interaction layer has still never executed on CI (A15)

4.1.1
---
- qxribbon: make RibbonGridContainer's enter event an override again on Qt 6.
  Qt changed the parameter of that virtual from QEvent to QEnterEvent, so the
  Qt 5 signature did not override anything there - it hid the virtual, and the
  handler silently never ran, which left a ribbon menu with two entries looking
  current. The declaration is guarded by the Qt version now and both variants
  carry an override specifier, so the same mistake fails to compile instead.
  This is the one library change of the batch, and qxribbon follows it:
  0.10.1 -> 0.10.2
- qxdock: keep a widget auto hidden when it is added to a window that has
  nothing else open. The panel being filled was toggled open before it had ever
  been shown, and the dock widget read that as "this is pinned and there is
  nothing else open, so unpin it": the auto hide container was deleted on the
  spot, the widget came back as an ordinary docked one, and the panel created
  for the container was left behind in the root splitter. The leftover also put
  contentRect() on the splitter's never laid out default of 640x480 before the
  first layout, which the auto hide resize limit and the choice of side bar
  both read. Only the .cpp changed, so no library moved for it
- ci: generate the moc that qxpropertyeditor.cpp includes before clang-tidy
  looks at it, when that file is part of the change. The file defines its
  Q_OBJECT classes in the .cpp and includes its own .moc, so the analysis used
  to stop at that include and report nothing - which cannot be told apart from
  a clean run
- docs: add the seventh known limitation, the frameless window on Windows and
  Qt 6 that cannot be maximised on its first show, with a NOTE at the restore
  branch of framelesshelper.cpp pointing at it
- libs: only qxribbon moved (0.10.2) - ribboncontainers.h is the one public
  header this batch touched. The other six stand still, and the qxdock fix
  above lives in that library's Unreleased section because its interface did
  not change

4.1.0
---
- qxdock: scroll the active tab back into view. The code that did it sat
  commented out because the written form ended the process: a QTimer::singleShot
  callback captured the private pointer `q` by reference, so it read a stack slot
  that updateTabs() had already released. Rewritten to capture by value, guard
  the tab with a QPointer and coalesce the scrolls, with regression tests for
  both the behaviour and the crash
- qxribbon: start the frameless helper at the window's real DPI instead of a
  hardcoded 96. The pair of DPI slots is a "did the DPI change" flag, and
  starting both at 96 made the first real change look like no change at all -
  so the one moment that needs the frame repaired was the moment the flag
  suppressed it. Falls back from GetDpiForWindow to the desktop DC to 96
- build: take a source file added to the tree into account on the next build. A
  glob caches its result at configure time and AUTOMOC keeps its own list of
  what it has scanned, so neither noticed a new file - the add_qtc_* helpers now
  clear the autogen stamp at configure time and the globs use CONFIGURE_DEPENDS
- project: give the 16 TODO/FIXME notes in qxdock, qxribbon and qxapp a verdict
  each (2 fixed, 9 written up as limitations, 5 removed as questions that do not
  stand); the 8 inherited from Qt in qxwindow are not claimed
- docs: add doc/pages/limitations.md - six behaviours that are known and
  deliberate, each with its cause, why it stays, and what changing it would take
- ci: report clang-tidy findings on the lines a change touched rather than on
  every line of the files it touched, and put the coverage listing in the job
  summary; neither is a gate (K8), but a measurement worth taking has to be
  measured honestly first
- libs: no library moved this time - the public headers are untouched, and a
  version is a claim about an interface

4.0.0
---
- project: remove qmake for good; BREAKING: the 88 .pro / .pri files are gone,
  the four demos that only qmake still built are gone, and the examples that
  were qmake-only are rebuilt with CMake - building QtCanpool now means CMake
  and nothing else, see doc/pages/migration.md for what to change
- project: build the examples with CMake under WITH_EXAMPLES (on by default);
  22 of them recovered, with the target names they had under qmake
- qxplugin: new library holding the plugin contract (QxPlugin, QxPluginSpec),
  discovery and dependency resolution (QxPluginManager, Kahn ordering, cycles
  and missing dependencies reported as a named chain), the three-step lifecycle
  with failure isolation, the surface a plugin programs against
  (QxPluginContext) and the object pool that lets two plugins cooperate without
  either including the other's header (QxObjectPool)
- qxapp: add QxPluginManagerDialog - list, details, reverse dependencies and
  diagnostics, with the switches stored as deviations from the metadata so that
  the metadata stays the default
- qxapp: keep the shell out of the plugin contract; pluginContext() hands out a
  thin adapter, so the host's window class reaches no plugin, not even in a
  comment
- modules: add the src/modules sample layer (output, notebook, filetree) and an
  IDE-style demo assembled from them that names not one of them in its source
- project: normalize line endings to LF across the tree, with .gitattributes and
  a .git-blame-ignore-revs entry (see doc/ROADMAP-3.x.md, decision K20)
- test: cover the plugin runtime, the sample modules and the assembled demo
- docs: add the plugin guide, the 3.x -> 4.0 migration section, and the
  architecture and component pages for qxplugin
- libs: qxcore 0.1.0 -> 0.1.1, qxapp 0.0.1 -> 0.1.0, qxplugin starts at 0.2.0;
  qxtheme, qxwindow, qxribbon and qxdock did not move

3.3.0
---
- qxapp: add QxWorkspaceManager, which puts a name on what the shell already
  persisted - a workspace is the dock layout plus the current page, and never
  the window geometry, which belongs to the window
- qxapp: give QxAppShell the four workspace entry points a menu needs:
  workspaceManager(), workspaceNames(), saveWorkspace() and applyWorkspace()
- qxapp: give the status bar indicator a percentage mode; it is one widget with
  two modes now, busy and progress, and the last call wins
- demo: give named workspaces and the progress indicator a place in the ribbon
  of the AppShell demo
- test: cover the workspace manager and the progress indicator with unit tests
- docs: document named workspaces and the status bar progress

3.2.0
---
- project: remove the qcanpool library; BREAKING: the deprecated forwarding
  headers go with it, so the old QCanpool names no longer compile - 3.1 only
  warned, see doc/design/3.0-MIGRATION.md
- project: remove the qmake application template and the legacy qcanpool
  examples; qmake stays frozen, so nothing new was added to replace them
- qxcore: add QxTranslator to load, switch and enumerate translations
- qxapp: add QxToast and QxToastManager for in-application notifications
  (levels, timeout, hover to hold, stacking and eviction)
- qxapp: give QxAppShell its own toast stack plus a showToast() shortcut
- qxapp: add QxProperty and QxPropertyEditor, a key/value form generated from
  property descriptors
- qxapp: add QxSettingsDialog, a settings dialog that reads and writes a
  QxSettings object
- project: wire language switching, toasts and the settings dialog into
  projects/template and into scripts/new-project, so a generated application
  can do all three out of the box
- test: cover the translator, the toasts, the property editor and the settings
  dialog with unit tests
- docs: document i18n, toasts, the property editor kernel and the settings
  dialog
- docs: give the documentation home page a library version to project version
  table

3.1.0
---
- qcanpool: remove the legacy ribbon
  (ribbonbar/ribbonpage/ribbongroup/ribbonwindow/ribboncontainer); BREAKING: use qxribbon
  instead, see doc/design/3.0-MIGRATION.md
- qcanpool: drop the QCANPOOL_CONFIG_RIBBON build option
- qcanpool: remove the fancyribbon demo (superseded by the qxribbon demos)
- qcanpool: move the general-purpose legacy widgets to qxapp and rename them into the Qx
  namespace (FancyToolButton->QxToolButton, ExtensionButton->QxExtensionButton,
  MenuButton->QxMenuButton, MenuAccessButton->QxMenuAccessButton, TinyTabBar->QxTabBar,
  TinyTabWidget->QxTabWidget, TinyNavBar->QxNavBar); the old names stay as deprecated
  forwarding classes for one release
- qcanpool: remove the legacy window-chrome widgets; BREAKING: QuickAccessBar, FancyTitleBar,
  WindowLogo, WindowToolBar, MiniTabBar, MiniTabWidget, FancyBar, FancyTabBar,
  FancyTabWidget, FancyWindow and FancyDialog are gone without a same-name replacement,
  see doc/design/3.0-MIGRATION.md
- qcanpool: remove the fancydemo and the examples and manual tests of the retired widgets
- qxapp: adopt the widgets taken over from qcanpool (QxToolButton,
  QxExtensionButton, QxMenuButton, QxMenuAccessButton, QxTabBar, QxTabWidget, QxNavBar)
- ci: add a quality job (coverage report, ASan/UBSan, clang-tidy); the sanitizers
  found and fixed two pre-existing defects in qxdock
- ci: check that the installed SDK is consumable and that scripts/new-project
  produces a buildable project
- test: add an automated test suite for qxwindow
- build: add CMakePresets.json for the common configurations
  (qt6, qt6-ninja, qt5, coverage, sanitize, wasm)
- project: add scripts/new-project to scaffold a new application
- demos: give the ribbon demo a --screenshot switch that renders the window to a file and exits
- test: add a demo screenshot regression (render sanity on every run, opt-in golden
  comparison with QTCANPOOL_VISUAL_BASELINE=1, per-platform goldens)
- docs: update the migration guide for the 3.1 legacy cleanup

3.0.0
---
- qt: Qt6-first, primary support Qt 6.5 LTS / 6.8 LTS; best-effort Qt 5.15
- qt: drop Qt <= 5.14 and MSVC2017
- project: cmake is the primary build system; qmake frozen (read-only)
- project: unify C++ standard to C++17
- project: unify library naming to the qx* namespace (QxRibbon/QxWindow/QxDock/QxApp/QxCore/QxTheme)
- project: provide an installable CMake package, consumable with find_package(QtCanpool)
- project: build and publish a demo for Qt for WebAssembly (online demo)
- qcanpool: freeze the legacy ribbon (ribbonbar/ribbonpage/ribbongroup/ribbonwindow)
- qcanpool: mark the legacy API deprecated, add a migration guide
- qxribbon: the only ribbon implementation
- qxwidget: renamed and relocated to qxapp (application framework)
- qxapp: add AppShell (navigation + pages + dock area + status bar + splash + layout persistence)
- qxcore: new infrastructure library (QxSettings config, QxLogger logging)
- qxtheme: new theme engine library (palette + stylesheet, runtime switch, follow system)
- ci: add a cross-platform build matrix (Linux/Windows/macOS x Qt 5.15/Qt 6.8) and a clang-format gate
- test: expand the automated test suite (qxcore/qxtheme/qxapp/qxribbon/qxdock)
- docs: add an API reference site (Doxygen), component guides and a 2.x -> 3.0 migration guide

